insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-allocation', 1, 'What kind of object is an allocation matrix?', '["A measurement, since it is derived from well coordinates that are the best known numbers in the field.", "A hypothesis about subsurface connectivity, which every analysis built on it inherits entirely.", "A constraint, since its rows are required to sum to one.", "A calibration, since its fractions are fitted to the observed producer responses and updated whenever new rate data arrives."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Every allocation matrix is a claim that could be wrong, so a conclusion resting on it should be stated with it attached: on the current allocation, the North element is over-injected. If moving a fraction from 0.45 to 0.40 flips your recommendation, you have a restatement of your assumption rather than a recommendation. Rows sum to AT MOST one, and the shortfall is meaningful.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-allocation', 2, 'Capacitance resistance modelling fits producer rates as a response to injector rates and reads connectivity from the fit. What is its great attraction, and its fragility?', '["It needs only well locations, but it assumes a homogeneous sand between every well pair.", "It needs only a geological model, but it is only as good as that model.", "It needs only a tracer return, but the return gives arrival times rather than volume fractions.", "It needs only rate data, but it cannot identify coefficients when the injectors move together."]'::jsonb, 3, 'No geological model is required, which is why CRM is attractive on a field with no simulation. It needs long records with genuine rate variation to identify the coefficients, and Ekene is exactly the case where it cannot: the two injectors take a fixed 0.6 to 0.4 split of the same total. Locations belong to the geometric method, a model to streamlines, and a return to a tracer.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-allocation', 3, 'Why do allocation matrices persist as judgement rather than being measured directly?', '["A tracer takes months to years to return, costs real money, gives arrival rather than volume fractions, and describes the path the tracer took rather than the path the bulk of the water takes.", "Because regulators require an allocation to be a stated assumption rather than a measurement.", "Because tracers cannot be injected into a well that is already on injection.", "Because a measured allocation would change month to month, while an analysis needs a single fixed matrix for the whole record."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Tracers come closest and are the strongest evidence available, and all four of those limitations are real. The honest framing is that a matrix is the best available answer rather than the right one. Tracers are routinely injected into live injectors, and a genuinely time-varying allocation would be valuable rather than an obstacle.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-allocation', 4, 'The engine validates, allocates and builds pattern periods. What does it refuse to do?', '["Allocate a row whose fractions sum to less than one, since the missing water cannot be assigned.", "Build pattern periods for overlapping patterns.", "Invent a matrix where none exists.", "Report an out-of-zone total, since that quantity is an inference rather than a measurement and belongs to the caller."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A pattern with no allocation routed to it gets no analysis at all, and the returned object says so with a reason: not an even split, not a distance-weighted guess, nothing with an explanation. Rows summing below one are allocated normally with the shortfall booked out of zone, overlapping patterns are handled, and the out-of-zone total is exactly what the engine reports.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-allocation', 5, 'Ekene''s matrix sends nothing at all from Ekene-2 to Ekene-5, at 1767.0597047072292 m, but keeps a 0.10 share from Ekene-4 to Ekene-5 at 2088.06130178211 m. Why the asymmetry?', '["Giving Ekene-5 nothing from either injector would assert more confidence than the data supports, since it is the only producer in the west.", "Because Ekene-4 has the higher injectivity index, so its water travels further.", "Because Ekene-2''s row was already full.", "Because the inverse-distance weighting is applied per injector and normalised separately, so the longer distance from Ekene-4 receives the larger residual share."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Ekene-2 to Ekene-5 connection crosses the whole field past two other producers and rounds to zero, while the Ekene-4 share is kept nonzero so that the western producer is not asserted to be completely unconnected. Both are judgement calls stated explicitly. Ekene-2''s row sums to 0.90 and had room, and injectivity does not set travel distance.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-allocation', 6, 'Ekene''s allocation fractions are rounded to the nearest 0.05. What does that buy and what does it cost?', '["It buys numerical stability in the conservation audit, at the cost of a small residual.", "It buys a matrix whose rows sum to one, at the cost of losing the out-of-zone information.", "It signals that the numbers are judgement, at the cost that re-deriving them from the stated method will not reproduce them exactly.", "It buys agreement with the injection split of 0.6 and 0.4, at the cost of tying the matrix to a design input that may change."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A matrix printed to three decimals invites a reader to believe there is a measurement behind the third one, so rounding to the precision you can defend is a good habit. The cost is real: state the method AND the matrix, and treat the matrix as authoritative. The audit residual is exactly zero regardless of rounding.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-allocation', 7, 'The North element is named for Ekene-2 and contains Ekene-1 and Ekene-6. How much of Ekene-4''s water does it receive?', '["None at all, since the element is defined by Ekene-2 and cross-feeding between the elements is excluded by construction.", "0.35, since a pattern''s injection is the allocation-weighted share of EVERY injector rather than of its namesake.", "0.75, which is the share Ekene-2 sends north.", "0.15, which is the share Ekene-2 sends south, mirrored across the two elements."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Ekene-4 sends 0.35 to Ekene-6, which is in the North element, so the North element receives 0.35 of Ekene-4''s volume as well as 0.75 of Ekene-2''s. The names are labels rather than claims that each element is fed by one injector, and cross-feeding is normal and handled directly. 0.75 and 0.15 are Ekene-2''s north and south shares.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-allocation', 8, 'What is robust to the choice of allocation matrix on Ekene, and what is not?', '["Both are robust, since the distances are measured and any sane weighting preserves their ranking.", "Neither is robust, since the matrix is the entire content of the analysis.", "The size is robust because the row sums are fixed at 0.90 and 0.85; the direction is not, because a different exponent can reverse which element is better supplied.", "The direction of the imbalance is robust; its size is not."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Both injectors sit in the east, so the eastern producers get more water than the western ones under any sane matrix and the sign of the split is safe. How far apart 1.20 and 0.61 sit does depend on the fractions. Checking that robustness explicitly is what lets you state the direction with confidence and the magnitude with a caveat.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-allocation', 9, 'The engine grades a row summing above one as an error and a row summing below one as a warning. Why is that asymmetry correct?', '["Allocating more water than the injector produced has no physical reading, while a shortfall is a real destination for real barrels and belongs on the report.", "Because a row above one breaks the conservation audit while a row below one does not.", "Because rows above one are usually typing errors while rows below one are usually deliberate.", "Because the engine can renormalise a row below one onto the producers, but has no defensible way to remove water from a row above one."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A sum above one is arithmetic that cannot be true. A sum below one says water went out of zone, vertically, across a fault, down-dip into the aquifer, or through a channel behind casing, and forcing the rows to one would delete those barrels from the accounting by asserting they supported production. The engine explicitly does NOT renormalise, which is the point.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-allocation', 10, 'Ekene''s out-of-zone total is 26997.051246145966 bbl, which is 0.11999999999999997 of the injection. Why is the answer exactly twelve percent?', '["Because the two shortfalls of 0.10 and 0.15 average to 0.125, which rounds to 0.12 at the matrix''s own precision.", "Because twelve percent was the design target for out-of-zone loss.", "Because 0.6 x 0.10 + 0.4 x 0.15 = 0.12, the injection split combined with the two row shortfalls.", "Because the out-of-zone fraction is defined as one minus the mean row sum, and the mean of 0.90 and 0.85 is 0.875."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Each injector''s own shortfall applies to its own volume, and the injection split weights them, giving exactly 0.12. The tail in the sixteenth digit is binary arithmetic rather than a real deviation. A simple average of the two shortfalls would give 0.125, which is the mistake of applying one average shortfall to the field total instead of each injector''s shortfall to its own volume.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-allocation', 11, 'What are the two ways to be wrong about the out-of-zone shortfall, and what does each cost?', '["Too small breaks the conservation audit; too large makes the pattern injections exceed the field total.", "Too small distributes water onto producers that never received it and leaves a starved element looking supplied; too large starves every pattern and prompts more injection than is needed.", "Too small raises a validation error; too large raises a warning.", "Too small understates the field cumulative VRR; too large overstates it, since out-of-zone water is removed from the numerator."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Both errors propagate straight into the pattern ledgers and therefore into the recommendation. Neither breaks the audit, which conserves whatever matrix you supply, and neither changes the FIELD cumulative VRR at all, since the field ledger uses total injection and knows nothing about the matrix.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-allocation', 12, 'The conservation audit returns a residual of exactly zero rather than nearly zero. What does that establish, and what does it not?', '["That every injected barrel is booked either to a producer or to out-of-zone, and nothing about whether the shares are right.", "That the allocation matrix is internally consistent, so its rows sum correctly.", "That the pattern injections sum to the field injection.", "That the fractions were derived from a conserving method such as streamline counting, since an arbitrary matrix would leave a residual."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The audit is bookkeeping: allocated plus out-of-zone equals the input, by construction, for any matrix whatsoever. It cannot tell a good matrix from a bad one. The pattern injections do NOT sum to the field injection precisely because the out-of-zone volume is excluded from them, which is the second of the two checks on a real pattern set.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-allocation', 13, 'Ekene''s per-producer allocated volumes are Ekene-6 92239.92509099872, Ekene-3 56243.85676280411, Ekene-1 40495.57686921897 and Ekene-5 8999.01708204866 barrels. What is the striking feature?', '["The four totals sum to the field injection of 224975.42705121645 barrels.", "Ekene-3 receives more than Ekene-1 despite being further from both injectors.", "The ranking follows the distance ranking exactly, which confirms the inverse-distance method was applied consistently.", "Ekene-5 receives about a tenth of what Ekene-6 does."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Ekene-5 is the far western producer and receives 8999.01708204866 barrels against Ekene-6''s 92239.92509099872, so if the matrix is broadly right it is not being flooded in any meaningful sense. The four totals sum to 197978.37580507048 rather than to the field injection, because 26997.051246145966 barrels are booked out of zone.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-allocation', 14, 'A pattern has no allocation routed to its producers. What does the engine return, and why is that the right design?', '["An even split across the pattern''s producers, flagged in the warnings array so the assumption is visible.", "A null value with no explanation, which the caller must interpret.", "A withheld result with a reason, because a defaulted number cannot later be told apart from a computed one.", "The field-level result, since that requires no allocation and is the closest available answer to the question asked."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Withholding buys provenance, since every pattern number that exists came from a matrix somebody wrote; a prompt at the moment someone is actually looking at that pattern; and a clean audit, since a reviewer can ask which patterns have allocations and get a definite answer. An even split silently assumes the very thing under investigation.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-allocation', 15, 'You receive a withheld pattern result. Which response is NOT one the lesson recommends?', '["Supply the missing input: build the allocation, write it down, and note the method.", "Fall back to an inverse-distance allocation, since well coordinates are always available.", "Answer a different question, since the field-level ledger needs no allocation and is always available.", "Record the gap, since no pattern-level conclusion being available is a perfectly good line in a report."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Building a distance allocation is a fine thing for a person to do deliberately, with the method recorded, which is the first recommended response. What is wrong is treating it as a fallback the tool applies for you, because then nobody can tell afterwards whether a number was computed or defaulted. The distinction is who decided, and whether it was written down.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-pattern-vrr', 1, 'How does buildPatternPeriods compute a pattern''s production and its injection?', '["Both are summed over the wells named in the pattern, producers and the namesake injector alike.", "Production is summed over the pattern''s producers and injection over its namesake injector.", "Production is summed over the pattern''s producers only; injection is the allocation-weighted share of every injector.", "Production is summed over the pattern''s producers and injection is the field total scaled by the pattern''s share of field production, which is the only split that conserves the total."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The production side is a simple restriction to the pattern''s wells. The injection side is where the matrix does its work: for each injector, sum the fractions it sends to producers in this pattern and take that share of its volume. Restricting injection to the namesake injector ignores cross-feeding, which on Ekene is 0.35 of Ekene-4 flowing into the North element.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-pattern-vrr', 2, 'The North element''s January 2023 injection is 2825.7643895409715 bbl. Where does that come from?', '["0.75 x 2873.6587012281067 from Ekene-2 plus 0.35 x 1915.7724674854045 from Ekene-4.", "The full 2873.6587012281067 bbl that Ekene-2 injected that month, since Ekene-2 defines the element.", "0.6 of the field injection.", "The field injection of 4789.431168713511 bbl less the 0.10 that Ekene-2 books out of zone and the 0.15 that Ekene-4 books."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Ekene-2 sends 0.45 to Ekene-6 and 0.30 to Ekene-1, both in the North element, giving 0.75 of its volume; Ekene-4 sends 0.35 to Ekene-6, giving 0.35 of its volume. The 0.6 and 0.4 injection split decides how much each WELL took, which is a different question from where that water went.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-pattern-vrr', 3, 'Ekene''s field cumulative VRR of 1.034899536109 splits into 1.2024353717815623 and 0.6097477559533482. Why is the field number not a compromise between them?', '["It is the arithmetic mean of the two, which happens to be close to 0.906 rather than to 1.03.", "It is a ratio of summed voidages over a geometry the field ledger does not model, so it is an average rather than a value either element takes.", "It is a compromise, weighted by each element''s produced voidage.", "It is neither, since the field ledger uses total injection while the pattern ledgers exclude the out-of-zone volume, so the three numbers are not comparable at all."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The field number is correct and it answers a different question. It is not a mean of the two element ratios, and while it is a ratio of sums, the sums differ from the elements'' by the 26997.051246145966 barrels booked out of zone, which is exactly why the pattern injected voidages total 201937.9433211719 rb against the field''s 229474.93559224083 rb. Not comparable is too strong: they are comparable once you know what each includes.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-pattern-vrr', 4, 'findFillUp returns index 0, label 2023-01, startedAbove true for the North element. What does that mean?', '["Its cumulative VRR crossed 1.0 in the first period, which is the earliest a crossing can occur.", "Its fill-up is inherited from the field series, which crossed at index 11.", "Its cumulative VRR reached 1.0 only in the first period and fell below it afterwards, so the flag marks a crossing that did not persist.", "Its cumulative VRR was already at or above 1.0 in the earliest period, so the crossing is not in the record."]'::jsonb, 3, 'startedAbove distinguishes a genuine crossing inside the record from a series that was already above the threshold when the record began. The North element was in surplus from the beginning, so the moment it caught up either happened before the record starts or never happened at all. It stays above thereafter, ending at 1.2024353717815623.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-pattern-vrr', 5, 'The South element''s fill-up is null while the North element''s is index 0. Why is null not the same as zero?', '["Null says the cumulative VRR never reaches 1.0 anywhere in the record; index 0 says it was already there in the first period.", "Null is the engine''s representation of index 0 when startedAbove is true.", "Null means the element has no allocation.", "Null means the pattern has no produced voidage in some period, so the cumulative ratio is undefined and the search cannot complete."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The two answers are opposite: one element has never replaced what it took, on any window, at any point in three years, and the other has been in surplus since the first month of record. Conflating them would merge the starved element with the over-injected one. A pattern with no allocation returns a withheld result rather than a null fill-up.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-pattern-vrr', 6, 'One pattern containing all four producers, with every injector row summing to 1, reproduces the field series exactly at 1.034899536109. Why is that invariant worth testing?', '["Because it proves the allocation matrix is correct.", "Because it establishes that pattern VRR and field VRR use the same formation volume factors.", "Because it is the one case where the pattern machinery has a known right answer, so a failure isolates a bug in the allocation layer rather than in the physics.", "Because it is the degenerate case the engine falls back to when no pattern definitions are supplied at all."]'::jsonb, 2, 'With every producer in one pattern and no out-of-zone shortfall, the pattern ledger must reduce to the field ledger, and any disagreement is arithmetic in the new layer rather than a question about geometry. It says nothing about whether a REAL matrix is right, and the engine withholds rather than falling back when patterns are missing.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-pattern-vrr', 7, 'Ekene''s two pattern injected voidages total 201937.9433211719 rb against a field 229474.93559224083 rb, while the two productions total exactly 221736.43680913927 rb. What do those two facts check?', '["That the allocation rows sum to 0.90 and 0.85 respectively.", "That the producers partition the field, and that the injection shortfall equals the booked out-of-zone volume.", "That the pattern ledgers use the same frozen factor set as the field ledger.", "That the two elements do not overlap, which is a necessary condition for the injection totals to be comparable."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Production matching exactly says every producer is in one pattern and none in two. Injection falling short by precisely the out-of-zone total says the same quantity computed two ways agrees. Those are the two checks to run on any real pattern set, and both are about coverage rather than about the row sums or the factors.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-pattern-vrr', 8, 'What does splitting the field ledger into two elements NOT change?', '["The available actions, since the split introduces the injection rate as a lever alongside the total.", "The first hypothesis to test when pressure is falling at a field VRR near 1.03.", "Where you look for problems, since an over-injected element raises sweep questions and a starved one raises connectivity questions.", "The field''s pressure history."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The pressure track was built from field cumulative volumes and is untouched by how you group them: the reservoir still went to a trough at 2088.9530115439275 psia and recovered 34.4931292839633 psi. The total is unchanged too, and so is the physics. What changes is the available actions, the diagnosis, and where you look, which are exactly the other three.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-pattern-vrr', 9, 'With one VRR number the only lever is total injection rate. What does a second number add, and why is it usually the cheaper lever?', '["The split between the elements, which costs a valve setting, while increasing total injection costs water, power and disposal.", "The injectivity of each injector, which can be improved by a workover at lower cost than new injection capacity.", "The target VRR for each element.", "The out-of-zone fraction, which can be reduced by recompleting the injectors above the oil water contact, at a cost far below that of additional water handling."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Rebalancing moves water that is already being injected and paid for, so it is nearly free, while raising the total buys more water and more disposal. Injectivity and completion changes are genuine options that the injectivity module takes up, but they are not what having two VRR numbers rather than one gives you.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-pattern-vrr', 10, 'Ekene-5 receives 8999.01708204866 barrels over the record against Ekene-6''s 92239.92509099872, and has never produced water. What follows if the matrix is broadly right?', '["Ekene-5 is being flooded efficiently, since it has produced no water despite receiving injection.", "Ekene-5''s connection to Ekene-4 must be stronger than 0.10.", "Ekene-5 is not being flooded in any meaningful sense, so expecting a flood response there expects something nobody paid for.", "Ekene-5 has broken through into a thief zone, since water that arrives without appearing at the producer must be leaving the pattern."]'::jsonb, 2, 'About a tenth of the water at the nearest producer, and no water production at all, is consistent with a well that has barely been touched by the flood. The absence of water at Ekene-5 is an absence of flood rather than evidence of efficient displacement, which is precisely the distinction the water arrival module draws between an absence of data and a phenomenon that has not occurred.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-pattern-vrr', 11, 'The North element reads 1.2425079040670826 instantaneous, 1.240523853264427 rolling over three periods, and 1.2024353717815623 cumulative. What does that ordering say?', '["That the element is over-injected now but has been under-injected historically.", "That the element has been in surplus throughout and is currently a little more so than its life average.", "That the rolling window has not yet caught up with a recent change in injection.", "That the cumulative is dragged down by the early ramp, in the same way the field cumulative sits below its latest instantaneous value."]'::jsonb, 1, 'All three sit well above one, so the element has been in surplus since the record began, which is exactly what its fill-up flag of startedAbove true records. The cumulative sits slightly below the recent values because the element''s surplus has grown, not because it was ever in deficit. The field''s early ramp is a field-level feature and the North element never shared it.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-pattern-vrr', 12, 'Why is a recommendation that only rebalances between the two elements budget-neutral?', '["Because the field total is unchanged at 229474.93559224083 rb injected, whatever the split.", "Because the out-of-zone fraction of twelve percent applies equally to both elements.", "Because the two elements have nearly equal produced voidage.", "Because the pattern injected voidages already exclude the out-of-zone volume, so moving water between elements cannot change the field ledger."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Moving water from one element to the other does not change how much water is bought, pumped or disposed of. The two produced voidages are indeed close, 112596.66438357021 against 109139.77242556904 rb, but that is a coincidence of this field rather than the reason. The out-of-zone shares differ between the two injectors, at 0.10 and 0.15.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-pattern-vrr', 13, 'Why is ''reduce north, increase south'' not yet a conclusion?', '["Because the field is already at a cumulative VRR above one, so no correction is warranted at all.", "Because the engine clamps any scale outside 0.5 to 2.0, so the recommended change cannot be delivered.", "Because the two elements have different produced voidages, so equalising their VRRs would not equalise their injection.", "Because it rests entirely on the allocation matrix, which is a judgement."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The split, its size, and therefore the recommendation all inherit the eight fractions somebody wrote down. The direction is robust because both injectors sit in the east, but the action needs the matrix stated alongside it. The recommended South scale of 1.644053193714856 is inside the clamp, and a field cumulative above one is exactly the average that hides the problem.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-pattern-vrr', 14, 'The twelve percent booked out of zone is described as a booked quantity rather than a found one. What does that mean for recovering it?', '["It can be recovered by renormalising the allocation rows to sum to one, which reassigns the water to producers.", "Reducing it requires knowing where it goes, which nobody on this field does.", "It can be recovered by increasing injection.", "It cannot be recovered at all, since water that has left the pattern is permanently lost to the flood."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The matrix asserts the shortfall; nobody measured it. Without knowing whether it goes vertically out of zone, across a fault, down-dip into the aquifer or through a channel behind casing, there is no intervention to design. Renormalising would simply delete the quantity from the accounting, and water that has left this pattern is not necessarily lost to the field.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-pattern-vrr', 15, 'A pattern''s VRR uses the same voidage equation and the same conventions as the field ledger. What IS new at pattern level?', '["The formation volume factors, which are evaluated at the pattern''s own average pressure.", "The rolling window, which must be shortened at pattern level.", "The allocation matrix, which is where all of the new information and all of the new uncertainty sits.", "The classification of wells, since a producer supported by two injectors belongs to two patterns and must be counted fractionally in each."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Nothing new is being measured: the same measurement is being partitioned. The arithmetic is identical and the conventions are inherited, so the matrix is not a detail on the side of the analysis, it is the entire content of it. Ekene''s two patterns do not overlap, and pattern membership is a list of producers rather than a fractional assignment.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-advice', 1, 'recommendPatternInjection scales injection by target over current VRR. Which current VRR does it use, and why?', '["The cumulative VRR, because pressure responds to accumulated net withdrawal rather than to a recent window.", "The rolling VRR, because the cumulative would try to correct the whole accumulated deficit in a single step.", "The instantaneous VRR, since a one-step correction should be based on the most recent period alone.", "Whichever of the three is furthest from the target, so that the recommendation is conservative in the direction of the correction."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A cumulative-based scale asks the element to make up three years of deficit at once. On the South element the two are close enough that the difference is a near-miss rather than an obvious failure, which is worse, because it looks right. The instantaneous is too noisy on a real field to drive a rate change from.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-advice', 2, 'The South element''s rolling VRR is 0.6082528252875008 and its current allocated injection is 1631.9738070945923 bbl per period. What does the rule recommend?', '["992.6526789604845 bbl per period, from inverting the scale as current over target rather than the other way.", "1631.9738070945923 bbl per period unchanged, since the scale falls inside the clamp band.", "3263.9476141891846 bbl per period, which is the clamped result at the maximum scale of 2.0.", "2683.051749612857 bbl per period, at a scale of 1.644053193714856."]'::jsonb, 3, '1631.9738070945923 x 1.644053193714856 = 2683.051749612857, an increase of roughly two thirds. The element is starved rather than over-injected. The clamp band spans 0.5 to 2.0, so a scale of 1.64 passes through unchanged rather than being clamped, and 3263.9476141891846 is what a target of 3.0 would give after clamping.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-advice', 3, 'The scale rule assumes VRR responds proportionally to injection. In what sense is that true and in what sense false?', '["It is exactly true of the arithmetic, since injected voidage is linear in Wi and the denominator does not contain Wi, and false of the reservoir, where more injection eventually means more produced water.", "It is true of the reservoir at steady state and false in the short term, since the pressure response lags the rate change.", "It is true for an increase and false for a decrease.", "It is false in both senses, since the out-of-zone fraction absorbs part of any increment before it reaches the pattern ledger."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The bookkeeping is linear by construction. The reservoir is not: extra water eventually raises the produced voidage through the water it brings back, which lowers the VRR again. So the rule is a one-step correction rather than a steady-state solution. The out-of-zone fraction scales with the increment and so does not break the proportionality of the arithmetic.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-advice', 4, 'The South element''s recommended increase splits as Ekene-4 from 1125.4991773066156 to 1850.3805169743844 and Ekene-2 from 506.4746297879769 to 832.6712326384727. Why does Ekene-2 appear at all?', '["Because the engine distributes any recommended change across all injectors in proportion to their injection split of 0.6 and 0.4.", "Because Ekene-4 alone cannot deliver the increment.", "Because Ekene-2 sends 0.15 of its water to Ekene-3, which is in the South element.", "Because the out-of-zone shortfall must be made up from the injector whose row sums highest, which is Ekene-2 at 0.90."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A pattern''s injection is the allocation-weighted share of every injector, so a change to the pattern''s injection is delivered by every injector that feeds it, in proportion to its own share. The 0.6 to 0.4 split governs how much each well takes in total rather than how a pattern correction is apportioned, and the engine''s arithmetic knows nothing about injectivity limits.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-advice', 5, 'The engine reports per-injector deltas for each pattern separately rather than netting them across patterns. Why is that the right choice?', '["Because the deltas are computed at different rolling windows for each pattern, so they are not commensurable.", "Because the two recommendations answer two different questions, and netting them would hide that one element is being cut while the other is raised.", "Because netting would break the conservation audit.", "Because a netted delta could exceed the injector''s clamp band even when neither pattern''s own scale does."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Ekene-2 is asked to reduce by 490.99914208304875 for the North element and to increase by 326.1966028504958 for the South, and the net is far less informative than the pair. Both use the same window, netting does not touch the audit, and the clamp is applied to a pattern scale rather than to an injector''s total.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-advice', 6, 'Ekene''s rebalance is described as nearly free. What makes it so?', '["Most of the correction is redistribution rather than volume: at a common target of 1.05 the total allocated injection rises by only about 13.64 percent.", "The valves are already installed on both injectors, so no capital is required.", "The two elements have nearly equal produced voidage.", "The water is already being paid for, so the whole correction is delivered at zero additional operating cost."]'::jsonb, 0, 'At a target of 1.05 the North element needs a cut of 15.358338556978579 percent and the South an increase of 72.6255853400599 percent, and those largely offset, leaving a modest net rise. That is the general reason pattern analysis pays for itself. The correction is not literally free, since the total does rise.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-advice', 7, 'The clamp is scale = min(2.0, max(0.5, target/current)). Why does the lesson say the flag matters more than the clamp?', '["Because the clamp is applied after the per-injector deltas, so the flag is the only place the limit is visible.", "Because the clamped value is still delivered.", "Because the clamp bounds are configurable while the flag is not, so only the flag has a fixed meaning across runs.", "Because a clamped result says the request was outside what the rule can defend."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The clamped NUMBER is arbitrary: it is the boundary of the band rather than an answer to the question asked. What carries information is that the request hit the boundary at all, which says the element is far enough from target that a single proportional step is not a sensible correction. The clamp is applied to the scale before the deltas are computed.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-advice', 8, 'At what target does the South element''s scale reach the clamp boundary of 2.0?', '["1.0, since any target at or above one requires more than doubling for an element at 0.61.", "2.0, since the scale equals the target when the current VRR is close to 0.5.", "1.2165056505750016", "3.0, which is the target the lesson uses to demonstrate the clamp in action."]'::jsonb, 2, '2.0 x 0.6082528252875008 = 1.2165056505750016, so any target above that is clamped. A target of 1.0 gives a scale of 1.644053193714856, comfortably inside the band, and a target of 3.0 gives a raw scale of about 4.93 which is clamped to 2.0 and reported with clamped true.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-advice', 9, 'At a common target of 1.05 the two elements converge on injection rates of about 2810 and 2817 barrels per period. What does that convergence reflect?', '["That the two elements are equally well connected to the injectors, which validates the allocation matrix.", "That the two elements produce similar voidage, which is a useful sanity check on the arithmetic.", "That the clamp has been reached on both elements.", "That the target of 1.05 was chosen so that the two elements would balance, which is why it differs from the field target of 1.0."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Injection at a target VRR is the target times the produced voidage, so two elements producing similar voidage need similar injection to hit the same target. 112596.66438357021 rb against 109139.77242556904 rb over the record is close. It says nothing about connectivity, neither scale is clamped, and 1.05 is the field''s own design target.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-advice', 10, 'Which is NOT a reason a common target across both elements may be wrong?', '["That the two elements have different produced voidages, so a common target implies different injection volumes.", "That the elements have different jobs: one holds the wells actually producing and responding, the other a well that has never seen water.", "That the elements have different deliverability, since raising the South means pushing harder on the injector whose injectivity degrades.", "That the out-of-zone risk differs, since Ekene-4 already books the larger shortfall at 0.15."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Different produced voidages are exactly what a VRR target normalises away, and here the two are close in any case. The other three are real arguments: what the pressure support is worth, whether it can be delivered, and whether the marginal barrel goes where the average barrel goes.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-advice', 11, 'What is the argument that differentiated targets double count a belief?', '["Because a differentiated target changes the rolling window, which already reflects the element''s recent behaviour.", "Because the clamp already limits how far an element can be moved.", "If you believe the South element is less connected, that belief belongs in the allocation matrix, which already encodes it once.", "Because the pattern periods already exclude the out-of-zone volume, which is where the connectivity belief is expressed."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The matrix is where connectivity judgements live. Applying the same belief a second time as a lower target for that element counts it twice, and the resulting recommendation is harder to audit because the belief is now in two places. Simplicity also has real value: a single field target is easier to operate against and harder to let quietly drift.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-advice', 12, 'The scale rule knows nothing about injectivity, pump capacity or fracture pressure. What does the lesson conclude from that?', '["That the rule should be replaced by one that solves for the achievable rate directly.", "That the recommendation is arithmetic and a separate check is needed before it becomes deliverable.", "That the clamp bounds of 0.5 and 2.0 stand in for those limits.", "That the recommendation should be applied only to reductions, since a reduction is always deliverable while an increase may not be."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A recommendation that cannot be delivered within the pressure limit is not a recommendation, and the injectivity module supplies the measurement that tests it. The clamp is a guard against an unreasonable proportional step rather than a proxy for a physical limit, and simplicity is precisely why the rule is not made to solve the whole problem at once.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-advice', 13, 'The North element is asked to reduce and the South to increase. Which injector is asked for the largest single increase?', '["Ekene-2, by 326.1966028504958 barrels per period into the South element, which is the larger of its two changes.", "Ekene-4, by 152.7552886480596 barrels per period, which is its share of the North element correction.", "Neither, since the North reduction of 490.99914208304875 exceeds every increase and the net change across both injectors is negative.", "Ekene-4, by 724.8813396677687 barrels per period into the South element."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Ekene-4 supplies 0.50 of its water to the South element and takes the larger part of the increase. That matters because Ekene-4 is also the well whose injectivity degrades from 2025-01-01, which the advice module does not know about. The net across both injectors is a modest increase rather than a reduction.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-advice', 14, 'The delta is a monthly rate change and the reservoir responds over months. What does the lesson advise?', '["Do not re-tune every month against a signal that has not arrived yet.", "Apply the delta gradually over the response time, so that the pattern VRR moves smoothly toward the target.", "Recompute the delta weekly.", "Apply the delta and then hold the injection fixed until the next pressure survey, since the survey is the only measurement that confirms the response."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The pattern VRR responds immediately in the bookkeeping and the reservoir responds over months, so a monthly re-tune chases the bookkeeping rather than the reservoir and can oscillate. The advice is about the cadence of re-tuning rather than about ramping the change itself, and waiting for a six-monthly survey would be far too slow a feedback loop.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-advice', 15, 'A target that is not a VRR at all could be used to set injection. What would such a target be?', '["A produced water volume, since that is what the facility is sized for.", "An injection rate.", "A pressure, since the VRR is only a proxy for the thing actually wanted.", "A recovery factor, since that is the quantity the flood exists to raise and the only one with an economic value attached."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Voidage replacement is a proxy for reservoir pressure, and where pressure is measured often enough it can be targeted directly, which removes the convention questions entirely. A rate is the lever rather than a target, water volume is a constraint, and a recovery factor is far too slow a signal to control injection against.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-injectivity', 1, 'The Hall plot puts I = sum p dt against W = sum q dt. What is the local slope?', '["Rate over pressure, which is the injectivity index and rises as the well improves.", "The cumulative injection per unit time, which is the average injection rate over the interval.", "Pressure over rate, which is a resistance.", "The product of pressure and rate, which is the hydraulic power delivered to the formation per barrel injected."]'::jsonb, 2, 'dI/dW is (p dt)/(q dt) = p/q, so a well needing more pressure for the same water has a steeper slope. It is the RECIPROCAL of an injectivity index rather than the index itself, which is why the Ekene above-reference slopes come out as 1/0.5 and 1/0.35.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-injectivity', 2, 'Why does the method integrate both series before differencing, rather than plotting p/q point by point?', '["Because daily rates jump around with facility operations and the ratio of two noisy series is noisier than either, while the slope of the integral over any interval is the time-weighted average p/q.", "Because the integral removes the dependence on reporting cadence, so wells reported daily and monthly become comparable.", "Because p/q is undefined whenever the rate is zero.", "Because the integral is the quantity the alert thresholds of 1.2 and 0.8 were calibrated against."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Integrating is a smoothing operation that preserves the physical meaning, and it turns a change of behaviour into a change of SLOPE, which is easier to see and to fit than a change of level. It does NOT remove the cadence dependence: the absolute magnitudes of I and W both scale with cadence, so compare slopes rather than integrals.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-injectivity', 3, 'A Hall curve that is flattening means falling resistance. Why is that often bad news?', '["Because a falling resistance means the injectivity index is falling, which reduces the volume the well can take.", "Because the usual causes are the formation parting and taking a fracture, or a channel opening behind casing into a thief zone.", "Because a flattening curve indicates the pressure gauge has drifted low.", "Because falling resistance raises the injection rate at fixed pressure, which pushes the pattern VRR above its target band."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Taking water more easily sounds like an improvement and usually is not, because the water is now going somewhere it was not going before. The engine raises an improving-injectivity alert at a slope ratio at or below 0.8 for exactly this reason. Falling resistance means a RISING injectivity index.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-injectivity', 4, 'The engine fits ordinary least squares slopes to the first and last thirds of the Hall points. What ratio triggers a declining-injectivity alert?', '["1.2 or above on the absolute wellhead pressure run only, since the above-reference run uses a tighter threshold of 1.05.", "0.8 or below, since a declining injectivity shows as a falling slope.", "Any ratio outside 0.8 to 1.2, since the engine does not distinguish the direction and reports a single injectivity-change alert.", "1.2 or above."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A ratio at or above 1.2 raises a declining-injectivity alert and one at or below 0.8 raises an improving-injectivity alert; between them the engine says nothing. Declining injectivity means RISING resistance and therefore a rising slope, and the two directions get distinct messages naming different plausible causes.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-injectivity', 5, 'An injector has rate data but no injection pressure. What does the engine do?', '["Returns it in a separate list of injectors without pressure, rather than building a plot on an assumed pressure.", "Builds the plot using the field average injection pressure for the missing periods.", "Excludes it silently.", "Builds the plot using the reference pressure of 2050 psia, which makes the slope exactly the reciprocal of the injectivity index by construction."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A point enters the sums only if BOTH pressure and rate are present, and a well with no pressure gets no plot and is named. That is the same habit as the withheld pattern advice and the producers excluded from the Chan table. Substituting a field average or the reference would produce a slope that looks like a measurement and is not.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-injectivity', 6, 'The engine excludes an injector with fewer than ten usable Hall points. Why does that minimum matter?', '["Because fewer than ten points cannot establish the reference pressure the plot is built on.", "Because the alert thresholds were calibrated on wells with at least ten points.", "Because the thirds construction needs enough points in each third for a least squares fit to mean anything.", "Because the Hall integral needs ten intervals before the time step floor of one day stops dominating the accumulated sums."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Four points per third gives slopes dominated by whichever point sits at the end. Ekene''s twelve points per third is reasonable. The reference pressure is a modelling choice made by the caller rather than something fitted from the data, and the one-day floor affects only pathological date sequences.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-injectivity', 7, 'Ekene''s injection pressure model is p = 2050 + q/II. What does building the Hall plot on p - 2050 give?', '["A slope of exactly II, since subtracting the reference inverts the resistance into a deliverability.", "A slope of exactly 1/II, independent of rate.", "A slope proportional to the injection rate.", "A slope of 1/II plus a small residual term from the reference subtraction, which vanishes only when the rate is constant."]'::jsonb, 1, 'With p_above = q/II the slope is (q/II)/q = 1/II exactly, with the rate cancelling. That is why Ekene-4''s baseline comes back as 2.0000000000000013 for II = 0.5 and its recent slope as 2.857142857142859 for II = 0.35, recovering the planted parameter to the last figure.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-injectivity', 8, 'Ekene-4''s above-reference slope ratio is 1.4285714285714286. What is that number exactly?', '["1 divided by 0.7, which is the fractional injectivity remaining after the degradation.", "The ratio of the recent to the baseline injection rate, since the well pushed harder to hold its volume target.", "2.857142857142859 minus 2.0000000000000013, expressed as a fraction of the baseline slope.", "0.5 divided by 0.35."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The ratio of the two slopes is (1/0.35)/(1/0.5) = 0.5/0.35, so it recovers the ratio of the two injectivity indices directly. It happens that 0.5/0.35 and 1/0.7 are the same number, but the second describes it as a remaining fraction rather than as what the slope ratio actually computes, and the rates barely change.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-injectivity', 9, 'Ekene-4''s injectivity drops on 2025-01-01, which is the start of the last 12 of 36 months. Why is the lesson explicit about that?', '["Because it is a construction convenience: a change starting mid-2024 would put both injectivities inside the last third and give a smaller ratio, possibly with no alert.", "Because the thirds construction requires the change to align with a third boundary or the fit fails.", "Because it means the ratio understates the true degradation.", "Because the engine chooses its thirds from the dates of any detected step change, so the alignment is a result rather than a coincidence."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The thirds construction detects a change that happens near the END of a record and is progressively blinder to one that happens earlier. On this fixture the alignment is exact and the recovered ratio is exact with it, which is convenient for teaching and would not hold on a real record. The engine''s thirds are fixed by point count rather than chosen from the data.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-injectivity', 10, 'Run on absolute wellhead pressure, Ekene-4''s slope ratio is 1.0669369155108472 and no alert is raised. Why is the absolute run diluted?', '["Because the absolute pressures are larger, so the least squares fit is dominated by the scatter in the pressure series.", "Because the absolute run integrates over the full record while the above-reference run uses only the degraded period.", "Because p/q = 2050/q + 1/II, and on Ekene-4''s average rate the reference term is about 26 against an injectivity term of 2.", "Because the reference of 2050 psia exceeds the reservoir pressure for part of the record, so the driving pressure is negative there and the two terms partly cancel."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Ekene-4 averages 78.44325711868179 bbl/d over the first third, so 2050/78.44325711868179 = 26.133540030068165 dominates the 1/II = 2 that carries the information. Degrading the well moves 1/II by 0.857 on a total near 28, which is a few percent and nowhere near the 1.2 threshold. Both runs use the same points.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-injectivity', 11, 'Same well, same month, same engine, and two defensible pressure conventions give one alert and none. What is the lesson?', '["That the absolute run is simply wrong and should never be used.", "That the convention has to travel with the number, because it decides whether the problem is visible at all.", "That the alert threshold of 1.2 is too high.", "That the engine should report both runs by default, since a diagnostic that depends on a convention is not a diagnostic."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Both readings of pressure are defensible and they disagree about whether the field has a problem, which is exactly why naming the convention is part of reporting the result. Lowering the threshold to catch the absolute run would raise false alerts on every well, since 2050/q varies with rate across the whole field.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-injectivity', 12, 'What does a Hall slope ratio NOT tell you?', '["The cause, whether it matters, or where between the gauge and the reservoir the resistance sits.", "The size of the change, since the ratio is compared against a threshold rather than reported.", "The direction of the change.", "Whether the well has enough data to support the fit, since the ratio is returned regardless of point count."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Rising resistance is consistent with scale, fines, bacterial plugging, injected solids, a partly closed valve or a gauge drift, and near-wellbore skin looks identical to a change far from the well. The alert message quotes the ratio precisely so a reader can judge the size, the direction is what separates the two alert types, and wells below ten points are excluded rather than reported.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-injectivity', 13, 'Ekene-4 kept hitting its volume target throughout its thirty percent injectivity loss. What does that show?', '["That the loss was not real, since a genuine thirty percent loss would have reduced the delivered volume.", "That the volume target was set below the well''s capacity from the start, which is why the degradation could be absorbed.", "That the Hall diagnostic is over-sensitive, since it raises an alert on a well that is meeting its operational commitments.", "That an injectivity loss only matters once it costs volume or the required pressure approaches parting."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The well pushed harder and delivered the same water, which is why the volume record shows nothing and only the pressure record shows the change. That is precisely what makes it worth finding early: the alert arrives while there is still headroom, rather than when the well starts falling short of its target.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-injectivity', 14, 'What are the blind spots of the thirds construction?', '["A change in the last third, a step change, and a record shorter than ten points.", "Missing pressure data, negative rates, and duplicate dates.", "A change in the middle third, a gradual trend, and a baseline period that was not itself representative.", "A change of injection rate, a change of reporting cadence, and a change in the reference pressure used for the subtraction."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A change confined to the middle third appears in neither fitted slope, a gradual trend spreads across both thirds and shrinks the ratio, and a baseline drawn over a period that was already abnormal compares against the wrong thing. Data quality problems are handled separately by the point requirements, and the ratio is by construction insensitive to rate.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-injectivity', 15, 'How should a Hall slope ratio be used well?', '["Report it to full precision, apply the thresholds strictly, and re-run monthly so that a trend can be established.", "Read it against the record length, look at the plot before believing it, and find the date of the change so it can be matched against operations records.", "Convert it to a percentage injectivity loss and track that instead.", "Compare it against the same ratio computed for the other injectors, since a field-wide change points to a water quality problem rather than a well problem."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A ratio of 1.3 on 36 monthly points is a year-on-year comparison while the same ratio on 12 points is a four-month comparison. Curvature and scatter carry information the two fitted numbers throw away. And a degradation with a DATE can be matched against a workover, a change of water source or a filter failure, which is what turns a diagnostic into an action.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-water-arrival', 1, 'The Chan diagnostic plots the water oil ratio and its time derivative on log-log axes. What distinguishes channelling from normal displacement?', '["Under channelling the WOR rises abruptly and then holds constant, while under displacement it rises steadily throughout.", "Under channelling the WOR keeps rising and so does its derivative, giving the derivative a clear positive slope; under displacement the WOR flattens and the derivative goes flat or declines.", "Under channelling the derivative is negative.", "Under channelling the WOR reaches a higher final value, while under normal displacement it stops below the economic limit, so that the two mechanisms are told apart by their magnitude rather than by their shape."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Water moving through a high permeability path keeps arriving in increasing proportion, so both the ratio and its derivative climb. Water arriving through the matrix as a front sweeps past saturates as the mechanism completes, and the derivative flattens or falls. It is a shape argument rather than a magnitude one, which is why Chan is about mechanism and not severity.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-water-arrival', 2, 'classifyChan reads a late-time derivative slope. What are its thresholds?', '["At or above 1.0 channelling and below 1.0 coning, with no intermediate category.", "At or above 0.4 coning and at or below 0.0 channelling, since a channel saturates while coning grows.", "At or above 2.0 channelling, since both Ekene producers exceed that and the field aggregate sits at 2.2114559940777454.", "At or above 0.4 channelling, at or below 0.0 coning, between them transitional, and null when it cannot classify."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The bands are 0.4 and 0.0 with a transitional region between them and a null when there is no series to classify. Ekene-6 at 2.348281726147951 and Ekene-3 at 1.6028659409443355 both sit far above the channelling threshold, but the threshold is 0.4 rather than anywhere near their values, and the directions are not reversed.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-water-arrival', 3, 'Which of Ekene''s producers get a Chan classification, and which do not?', '["Ekene-3 and Ekene-6 are classified; Ekene-5 has never made water and Ekene-1 has only six wet months against a ten-point minimum.", "All four are classified, with Ekene-1 and Ekene-5 returning transitional.", "Only Ekene-6, since it is the only producer to have broken through.", "Ekene-3, Ekene-6 and Ekene-1 are classified, while Ekene-5 is excluded for having no water production at all."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Two producers return a curve and two return nothing, and the two absences mean opposite things. Ekene-5 has zero wet months in 36, so there is no water oil ratio to plot at all. Ekene-1 started making water in June 2025 and has six wet months, which is below the engine''s minimum of ten points with a positive WOR.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-water-arrival', 4, 'Ekene-5''s absence and Ekene-1''s absence are both blanks in the Chan table. Why must they be read differently?', '["One is excluded by a data quality rule and the other by a minimum point count.", "One is a producer and the other an injector.", "One is a phenomenon that has not occurred and the other is a phenomenon that has started with too short a record.", "One returns null and the other returns a transitional classification, so only the first is genuinely absent from the table."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Ekene-5 is barely flooded and has made no water at all; Ekene-1 broke through recently and will be classifiable in a few more months. The general habit is to separate no data at all, not enough data yet, and data excluded by a quality rule, because each implies a different next action. Both are producers and both return no classification.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-water-arrival', 5, 'Why is the field-level Chan aggregate of 2.2114559940777454 the least useful of the three numbers?', '["Because it is computed from allocated rather than measured rates, so it inherits the allocation matrix.", "Because it is dominated by Ekene-6 and mixes producers with different mechanisms and different arrival dates into one curve.", "Because it falls between the two producer values.", "Because the field aggregate uses a longer smoothing window, which flattens the late-time slope and biases it toward the coning band."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A mechanism diagnostic on a sum of wells is a mechanism diagnostic on nothing in particular: Ekene-6 dominates the water, and averaging over wells that broke through at different times and may be behaving differently produces a curve no single physical process generated. Chan works entirely from measured producer rates and needs no allocation.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-water-arrival', 6, 'The lesson says the Ekene Chan classifications should be held loosely. Why?', '["Because the fixture''s water histories are designed ramps, and a rising ramp read on its rising limb produces a steep derivative whether or not a channel exists.", "Because the smoothing window is too short for 36 monthly points.", "Because both producers sit above the threshold, so the diagnostic cannot discriminate between them.", "Because the classification depends on the allocation matrix, which is judgement, so every conclusion in the module inherits that uncertainty."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Chan classifies from shape, and a designed ramp has a shape by construction. That is an honest caveat about a teaching fixture rather than a defect in the method, and it is exactly why the Expert tier''s independent volumetric back-out matters: it reaches the same conclusion without using any curve shape at all. Chan does not use the allocation.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-water-arrival', 7, 'Ekene-6 is 715.8910531638176 m from Ekene-2 and 989.9494936611666 m from Ekene-4, and was the first producer to make water, in March 2024. How does that sit with its Chan slope of 2.348281726147951?', '["It confirms channelling, since the nearest producer of the four breaking through first is precisely the signature of a preferential path.", "It contradicts channelling, since a channel would produce breakthrough much sooner than fourteen months.", "It is irrelevant, since Chan reads only the shape of the water oil ratio and distance plays no part in the classification.", "It is corroborating context rather than proof, since being nearest and first is consistent with both a channel and an ordinary front."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The nearest producer breaking through first is what an ordinary displacing front would also do, so the geometry does not discriminate. It is worth stating because it makes the reading coherent rather than because it adds evidence. Distance does not enter the classification, but that is a reason it is separate corroboration rather than a reason to ignore it.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-water-arrival', 8, 'The lag table reports Ekene-2 to Ekene-6 with a lag of 0 and Ekene-2 to Ekene-5 with a lag of 6, in a field named lag_days. What is the first problem?', '["The lags are reported in days but computed from monthly data, so each should be multiplied by 30.4375.", "The lag of 0 is a failure to converge.", "The lags count ROWS, and the rows are monthly, so these are months labelled as days.", "The lags are computed on the raw series rather than the detrended one, so a common trend inflates every correlation and drives the reported lag toward zero."]'::jsonb, 2, 'crossCorrelatePair steps through the series one ROW at a time, so a lag of 6 on monthly rows is six months. The field name says days. Multiplying by 30.4375 would be the right conversion only if you knew the rows were months, which is the point. The engine does detrend by first differences before correlating.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-water-arrival', 9, 'Every producer returns the same lag and a correlation identical to fifteen figures for both injectors. Why?', '["Because the two injectors are close enough together that their pressure signals are indistinguishable at the producers.", "Because the fixed 0.6 to 0.4 split makes the two injection series exact scalar multiples, and correlation is scale invariant.", "Because the engine correlates against the field injection total.", "Because 36 monthly points cannot resolve a difference in lag smaller than one period, and the true difference between the two injectors is less than a month."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The two series carry no information that could separate them: one is 0.6 of the total and the other 0.4 of the same total, and correlation does not see a scale factor. No amount of additional data fixes that, which makes it a structural limitation rather than a statistical one. The engine does correlate each injector separately, which is what makes the identical answers so telling.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-water-arrival', 10, 'The planted response order is Ekene-6 at 3 months, Ekene-1 at 5, Ekene-3 at 6 and Ekene-5 at 9. The table reports 0, 2, 3 and 6. What does it get right?', '["The ordering of the producers, and nothing else.", "The ordering and the spacing, since the reported lags are a constant offset below the planted ones.", "The absolute lags, once the row-to-month conversion is applied.", "Nothing: the reported lags are unrelated to the planted response times, which is why the table is described as the most instructive failure in the course."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The reported lags come out at about half the planted response times and the spacings do not match a constant offset, but the RANKING of the four producers is preserved. That is a real if modest result, and the honest summary is that the table ranks the producers and cannot attribute them to an injector.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-water-arrival', 11, 'The Chan derivative is computed on a smoothed water oil ratio series. Why smooth first?', '["Because the raw series contains zeros before breakthrough, whose logarithm is undefined.", "Because smoothing removes the seasonal variation in produced water.", "Because differentiating a noisy series amplifies the noise, and the late-time slope is fitted to the derivative.", "Because the engine''s minimum of ten points is applied after smoothing, so an unsmoothed series would rarely reach it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Numerical differentiation is a noise amplifier and the classification rests on the slope of the derivative, so the derivative has to be usable. Points with a non-positive WOR are excluded rather than being taken through a logarithm, the minimum is applied to points with a positive WOR, and there is no seasonal term in a waterflood water cut.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-water-arrival', 12, 'Chan is described as being about mechanism rather than severity. What does that mean in practice?', '["A steeper slope means a more severe channel, so the classification and the severity are the same reading.", "A well at a WOR of 2 and a well at a WOR of 40 can carry the same classification, and the classification says nothing about how much water is being made.", "A classification is only valid once the WOR exceeds one.", "The classification is a category rather than a number, so two wells in the same category cannot be ranked at all by any means."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The late-time slope reads how the water oil ratio is EVOLVING, not how large it is. Severity is the WOR itself, the water rate, and the cost of handling it, all of which are separate numbers already in the report. Two wells in the same category can certainly be ranked, just not by their classification.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-water-arrival', 13, 'What would be the wrong way to act on a channelling classification at Ekene-6?', '["Commission a tracer from Ekene-2 to measure the connectivity directly.", "Check whether the water at Ekene-6 is injected water or formation water.", "Look for an independent line of evidence that does not depend on the shape of a curve.", "Increase injection into the North element to overcome the loss."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A channel is a preferential path and adding volume to a system with a preferential path adds volume to the path. The other three are all sound next steps: a direct measurement, a cheap falsification test, and a second independent argument, which is exactly what the Expert tier''s volumetric back-out supplies.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-water-arrival', 14, 'Which of the tier''s five findings are independent of the allocation matrix?', '["The Hall injectivity finding and the Chan water arrival finding, since both work from measured per-well pressure and rate data.", "The pattern VRR split and the recommended injection, since both are computed from measured volumes.", "The lag table alone.", "None of them, since every diagnostic in the tier is computed on periods built through the allocation."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Hall works from one injector''s own pressure and rate, and Chan from one producer''s own oil and water rates, so neither needs to know where the water went. The pattern split, the correction and the recommendation all rest on the eight fractions. The lag table also uses raw rates, but it is a failure rather than a finding.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-water-arrival', 15, 'What is the general lesson the lag table teaches, beyond Ekene?', '["That cross-correlation requires detrending, without which any two rising series correlate strongly.", "That field naming matters.", "That a technique can be correctly implemented and still be unable to answer the question, because the information is not in the data.", "That correlation does not imply causation, so even a strong injector-producer correlation cannot on its own establish a flow connection between them."]'::jsonb, 2, 'No amount of additional data would fix the injector attribution here: two proportional inputs cannot be separated by any method that sees only those inputs. Recognising when a question is unanswerable from the available data is worth more than the technique. Detrending and field naming are real issues the lesson also raises, but they are fixable ones.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 1, 'Ekene''s two elements produce 112596.66438357021 rb and 109139.77242556904 rb of voidage but receive 135390.21199942206 rb and 66547.73132174983 rb of injection. What is the one-line summary?', '["The two elements are balanced in production and differ only in the out-of-zone share of their feeding injectors.", "Fifty nine percent of the water goes to half the production and twenty nine percent to the other half, with twelve percent out of zone.", "The North element produces more and is injected more, in proportion.", "The South element produces slightly more voidage than the North, so the injection imbalance is even larger than the raw volumes suggest."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The productions are almost identical while the injections differ by a factor of two, which is what makes the split so stark. The North element produces marginally MORE voidage than the South, so the imbalance is not amplified by the production side, and the out-of-zone shortfall belongs to the injectors rather than to the elements.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 2, 'The South element''s cumulative deficit stands at 42592.04110381921 reservoir barrels. What is that quantity?', '["The total volume of water booked out of zone from the injector that feeds it.", "The difference between its produced voidage and the North element''s.", "The volume the recommendation would add over the remaining life of the flood at the recommended rate of 2683.051749612857 barrels per period.", "The injected voidage it would have needed to reach a cumulative VRR of 1.0 over the record, less what it received."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Its produced voidage is 109139.77242556904 rb and its injected voidage 66547.73132174983 rb, and the difference is the shortfall against full replacement. The out-of-zone total is 26997.051246145966 barrels across both injectors, the two productions differ by only about 3457 rb, and the recommendation is a forward-looking rate rather than a cumulative.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 3, 'Which of the tier''s findings depend on the allocation matrix, and which do not?', '["The pattern split, the correction and the recommendation depend on it; the Hall injectivity finding and the Chan water arrival finding do not.", "All five depend on it, since every diagnostic is computed on periods built through the allocation.", "None depend on it, since the matrix affects only the reporting labels.", "The Hall and Chan findings depend on it because they are computed per well, whereas the pattern split does not because it works from the field totals."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Hall works from one injector''s own pressure and rate and Chan from one producer''s own oil and water rates, so neither needs to know where the water went, which makes them the more robust half of the tier. Every pattern number rests on the eight fractions, and each conclusion should carry that condition explicitly.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 4, 'The correction at a field target of 1.05 is a 15.358338556978579 percent cut in the north and a 72.6255853400599 percent increase in the south. Why does that net to only about 13.64 percent more injection?', '["Because the two percentages are applied to allocated rather than raw injection, and allocation removes twelve percent from both.", "Because the north element is much the larger of the two.", "Because most of the correction is redistribution rather than volume, which is the general reason pattern analysis pays for itself.", "Because the percentages are computed against different baselines, so they cannot be added and the net figure comes from a separate calculation on the totals."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A large percentage increase on the smaller allocated volume and a smaller percentage cut on the larger one very nearly offset. The two elements are in fact close in size, receiving 135390.21199942206 rb against 66547.73132174983 rb, and while the percentages do have different baselines, that is a reason to compute the net from the totals rather than the explanation for why it is small.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 5, 'Capstone field 1 asks for the out-of-zone injection. What is the mechanism, and what is the likely mistake?', '["Subtract the pattern injected voidages from the field injected voidage; the mistake is working in reservoir rather than surface barrels.", "Multiply each row''s own shortfall by that injector''s own volume and total; the mistake is applying a single average shortfall to the field total.", "Multiply the field injection by the mean of the two row sums; the mistake is using the median instead.", "Total the allocated volumes per producer and subtract from the field injection; the mistake is omitting Ekene-5, whose share is small enough to round away."]'::jsonb, 1, 'For each row, injected volume times one minus that row''s sum, then total. On Ekene the fixed 0.6 to 0.4 split makes the average-shortfall shortcut agree by coincidence, but the habit is wrong and on a field with varying splits it would not. The check is that the answer is close to twelve percent of the total injected.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 6, 'Capstone fields 2 and 3 ask for the North and South cumulative VRRs. What is the likeliest way to get a plausible wrong answer?', '["Using only the namesake injector''s water for each element, which leaves North too low by roughly a third.", "Summing production over all four producers rather than over the pattern''s own two.", "Using the instantaneous rather than the cumulative series.", "Applying the frozen factor set rather than the pressure-tracked one, which moves both answers in the fourth decimal place."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The North element receives 0.75 of Ekene-2''s water AND 0.35 of Ekene-4''s, and using only Ekene-2 drops a substantial share. The check is that North should sit well above the field value and South well below, and that the two produced voidages should be within a few percent of each other. The factor convention moves the answer far inside the tolerance.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 7, 'Capstone field 4 asks for the South element''s recommended injection. Which two mistakes does the lesson name?', '["Using a target of 1.05 rather than 1.0, and applying the clamp when the raw scale falls inside the band.", "Using the instantaneous rather than the rolling VRR, and forgetting to convert to reservoir barrels.", "Using the North element''s rolling VRR by mistake, and applying the scale to the field injection rather than to the element''s.", "Using the cumulative rather than the rolling VRR, and scaling raw injector volumes rather than the allocated injection into the element."]'::jsonb, 3, 'On this element the cumulative and rolling values are close enough that the error is a near-miss rather than an obvious failure, which is worse because it looks right. And the baseline is the ALLOCATED injection into the pattern, 1631.9738070945923 barrels per period, rather than the raw volumes of the injectors that feed it. The capstone states its target as 1.0.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 8, 'Capstone field 5 asks for the Hall slope ratio on Ekene-4. What does the field exist to test?', '["The thirds construction, since the degradation aligns exactly with the last third of the record.", "The minimum point count, since Ekene-4 has 36 monthly points against a minimum of ten.", "The pressure convention, since the absolute run gives 1.0669369155108472 and the above-reference run gives the graded answer.", "The integration step, since the Hall integral''s absolute magnitude depends on the reporting cadence used while its fitted slope does not."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The prompt states the 2050 psia reference explicitly, so reading it is part of the exercise: same well, same month, same engine, and the convention decides whether a thirty percent degradation is visible at all. The check is that the answer should be recognisable as a simple ratio of two injectivity indices, with the fitted slopes their reciprocals.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 9, 'Capstone field 6 asks for the Chan late-time slope on Ekene-6. What are the two likely mistakes?', '["Using a smoothing window that is too short, and including the pre-breakthrough zeros in the log-log fit.", "Fitting the slope of the WOR itself instead of its derivative, and reading the field curve rather than the Ekene-6 producer curve.", "Reading Ekene-3''s curve, and using the early-time rather than the late-time slope.", "Applying the classification thresholds before fitting, and reporting the category rather than the slope value."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Both mistakes return a plausible number rather than an obvious failure, and the field curve is close enough to Ekene-6''s to look right. The check is that the answer should sit well above the 0.4 threshold and be the LARGER of the two producer slopes, since only two producers get a curve at all.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 10, 'Why is the lag table called the most instructive failure in the course?', '["Because it is correctly implemented, returns a confident-looking answer, and cannot answer the question it appears to answer, for a reason no extra data would fix.", "Because it contains an arithmetic error in the detrending step that inverts the sign of the reported lag.", "Because it returns null for every pair.", "Because its correlations fall below the significance threshold, so the engine suppresses the table and the absence itself is the lesson."]'::jsonb, 0, 'It reports lags in rows and calls them days, it returns identical answers for both injectors because their series are exact scalar multiples, and its lag values come out at about half the planted response times. It does get the ordering right. Recognising when the information is simply not in the data is worth more than the technique.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 11, 'The Expert tier asks a question neither of the first two tiers can. What is it?', '["Whether the allocation matrix is correct, which the Expert tier settles with a tracer.", "Whether the field''s pressure will hold at the current injection rate over the next decade.", "How much of the reservoir the water is actually contacting, and how much oil it removes from the rock it passes through.", "Which injector supports which producer, which the Expert tier resolves by combining the lag table with the pattern ledgers."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Everything so far is a volume balance or a diagnostic on a rate history, and none of it contains a model of the displacement. The Expert tier builds vertical sweep, areal sweep and a rate-time forecast, importing displacement efficiency from the SCAL course. It does not settle the allocation and it cannot separate the two injectors either.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 12, 'What changes about the nature of the judgement as you move from the Associate tier to the Professional tier to the Expert tier?', '["Judgement in the model, then judgement in the inputs, then objective numbers, as each tier removes an assumption from the one before it.", "The judgement is constant across the three, and only the volume of data changes.", "Objective numbers throughout, since every tier is grounded in the same committed fixture and the same validated engines.", "Objective numbers, then judgement in the inputs, then judgement in the model."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The Associate tier counts barrels with stated conventions. This tier adds eight allocation fractions somebody wrote down. The Expert tier adds piston displacement, no crossflow, equal porosity per layer, piston-like areal growth and no interference between patterns. The engines surface those as warnings rather than hiding them, and reading the warnings is part of the work.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 13, 'The Expert tier''s channelling result uses the allocation to decide how much water reached Ekene-6. How should that dependence be stated?', '["As fatal, since a result that depends on a judgement cannot be reported as a finding.", "As real but not decisive, since the fraction would have to be wrong by a factor of about twenty to change the story.", "As irrelevant, since the back-out is a volumetric argument.", "As unquantifiable, since no sensitivity can be computed without a second and fully independent allocation to compare it against."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A different matrix gives a different swept fraction, so the dependence is real and should be stated. It is also quantifiable directly: giving Ekene-6 the entire field injection moves the answer from about 1.47 to 3.58 percent, still percent-level. Being a volumetric argument does not exempt it, since the allocation supplies its numerator.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 14, 'What structural lesson does the tier ask you to look for in your own work?', '["Two independent methods beating one good method, since Chan argues from the shape of a curve and the back-out from a volume, and neither would be conclusive alone.", "A single well-validated method applied consistently, since two methods disagreeing leaves you with no answer at all.", "A measurement wherever a judgement currently stands.", "A model whose assumptions are all satisfied, since a screening tool used outside its assumptions produces results that cannot be interpreted at all."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Two hypotheses failing in the same direction from disjoint inputs is a much stronger position than either alone. Disagreement between two methods is more informative still, and the reconciliation is usually where the real answer is. Replacing every judgement with a measurement is rarely affordable, and the Expert tier''s most valuable result comes precisely from a model used where one of its assumptions is violated.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 15, 'The engines print warnings such as vertical sweep applied as a constant multiplier on the flooded volume. What are those warnings for?', '["They flag that an input failed validation, so the result should be treated as provisional.", "They record which assumptions the particular dataset violates.", "They are the tool stating the edge of its own competence, and reading them is part of the tier.", "They mark results that are unsuitable for reporting, so a run producing warnings should be repeated with corrected inputs."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The strings are unconditional statements of the model''s standing assumptions and they ride along with valid results, so a report that quotes a number without them has stripped the caveats from it. They are not conditional on the data, not a validation failure, and not a grading of the run, since every run of these engines carries them.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 1, 'You report that the North element is over-injected. A colleague disagrees. What is the first question to settle?', '["Whether they used the rolling or the cumulative VRR, since the two give different answers on this element.", "Whether they are working in reservoir or surface barrels, which differ by the formation volume factors.", "Whether they disagree with the analysis or with the allocation matrix.", "Whether they applied the operator target band of 1.00 to 1.20 or the generic interpretation bands at 0.9 and 1.1."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Those are two different conversations and confusing them wastes both. That is why a pattern conclusion should be stated with its allocation attached: on the current allocation, the North element is over-injected. The series choice and the band are visible in the report, and both tiers work in reservoir barrels throughout.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 2, 'The geometric allocation method is available on day one, before any dynamic data exists. What does it use, and what is the corresponding weakness?', '["Well locations, which are the best known numbers in the field, but it knows nothing about permeability trends or well shut-ins.", "Rate histories, which are recorded continuously, but it needs long records with genuine variation.", "A geological model, which encodes the permeability trends, but it is only as good as that model.", "Tracer returns, which measure connectivity directly, but the return takes months to years and gives arrival rather than volume fractions."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Distance weighting is cheap, transparent and reproducible, and it uses the one input nobody argues about. What it cannot see is anything dynamic. Rate histories belong to capacitance resistance modelling, a geological model to streamline allocation, and tracer returns to a direct measurement.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 3, 'Ekene''s matrix is rounded to the nearest 0.05. What does the lesson say about reproducing it from the stated method?', '["You will reproduce it exactly, since inverse distance weighting on the mapped coordinates is deterministic.", "You will reproduce it only if you use the same exponent, which is why the exponent must be stated alongside the fractions.", "You should not attempt to, since the fractions are judgement and the stated method is only an illustration of how they were first sketched.", "You will not reproduce it exactly, so state the method and the matrix, and treat the matrix as authoritative."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The rounding breaks the exact correspondence with any weighting rule, which is a real cost of a habit that is otherwise good. The method belongs in the report so a reader can judge the reasoning, and the matrix is what the analysis actually used. Stating the exponent helps but does not restore exactness once the rounding is applied.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 4, 'Ekene-2 sends 0.75 of its water to the North element and 0.15 to the South, while Ekene-4 sends 0.35 north and 0.50 south. What does that pattern show?', '["The matrix is inconsistent, since an element named for one injector should receive the majority of that injector''s water.", "Cross-feeding between elements is normal, and an element''s name is a label rather than a claim about which injector feeds it.", "The two elements overlap, since both receive water from both injectors.", "The injection split of 0.6 to 0.4 has been applied twice, once to the well volumes and again to the element shares."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Both injectors send water to both elements and the engine handles that directly: a pattern''s injection is the allocation-weighted share of EVERY injector. Overlap would mean a PRODUCER belonging to two patterns, which is not the case here. The 0.6 to 0.4 split and the allocation fractions answer different questions and are applied once each.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 5, 'A row summing to 1.2 and a fraction of -0.1 are both errors, while a row summing to 0.9 is a warning. What distinguishes the first two from the third?', '["The first two describe arithmetic that cannot be true, while the third describes a real destination for real barrels.", "The first two are detected before allocation and the third only afterwards.", "The first two can be corrected automatically by renormalising the row, while the third cannot.", "The first two would break the conservation audit, while the third leaves it intact at a residual of exactly zero."]'::jsonb, 0, 'You cannot allocate more water than the injector produced, and an injector cannot remove water from a producer''s support. A shortfall, by contrast, says the water went out of zone, vertically, across a fault, into the aquifer or through a channel behind casing. Renormalising is exactly what the engine refuses to do, because it would assert the water supported production.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 6, 'Ekene books 26997.051246145966 barrels out of zone. What kind of number is that?', '["A measurement, since it is the residual of the conservation audit against the metered injection.", "A design value, since the shortfalls of 0.10 and 0.15 were specified before the flood started.", "An assumption asserted by the matrix, since nobody measured it, and that should be stated loudly beside it.", "A bound, since the true out-of-zone volume cannot exceed the amount the allocation leaves unassigned."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Twelve percent of the injection budget going somewhere that produces nothing is a substantial number, and on this field nobody measured it: the matrix asserts it. The conservation audit conserves whatever matrix you supply and so cannot test it, and the true volume could easily be larger or smaller than the assumed one.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 7, 'What does withholding a pattern result buy, compared with returning an even split?', '["A smaller output object, since patterns without allocations are omitted entirely from the result.", "Provenance, a prompt at the moment someone is looking at that pattern, and a clean audit of which patterns have allocations.", "Protection against a conservation error, since an assumed split would not conserve the injected volume.", "Consistency with the field ledger, which also returns nothing when an input is missing rather than substituting a default."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Every pattern number that exists came from a matrix somebody wrote, so there is no path by which a number appears without an allocation behind it. The refusal arrives when someone is best able to supply the missing judgement, and a reviewer can ask which patterns have allocations and get a definite answer. An even split would conserve perfectly well; that is not the objection.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 8, 'A pattern is defined as a name and a list of producers. Why is the injector not part of the definition?', '["Because the injectors are already defined by the well classification, so naming them again would be redundant.", "Because a pattern may contain no injector at all, as the South element does.", "Because the injection split of 0.6 to 0.4 already assigns each injector''s volume, so a pattern needs only its production side.", "Because a pattern''s injection comes from every injector in proportion to what the allocation routes to its producers."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Naming an injector in the pattern would suggest the pattern is fed by it, and the North element takes 0.35 of Ekene-4''s water as well as 0.75 of Ekene-2''s. Both elements do receive water from both injectors, and the split governs how much each WELL took rather than where that water went.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 9, 'The North element''s cumulative VRR is 1.2024353717815623 and the South''s is 0.6097477559533482, against a field 1.034899536109. What does that say about the field number?', '["It is wrong, since a field average that matches neither element cannot describe the field.", "It is a correct answer to a different question: an average over a geometry the field ledger does not model.", "It is a weighted mean of the two, with the weights being each element''s produced voidage.", "It is an upper bound on the worse element and a lower bound on the better one, which is all a field-level number can ever provide."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The field number was never wrong; it was answering a different question than the one people were asking of it. It is not a mean of the two element ratios, and the pattern injected voidages exclude the out-of-zone volume so the three quantities are related but not by a simple weighting.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 10, 'findFillUp can return a crossing, a crossing flagged startedAbove, or null. Which does each Ekene element get?', '["North returns index 11 like the field; South returns index 0 with startedAbove false.", "Both return null, since neither element''s cumulative series crosses 1.0 inside the record.", "North returns index 0 with startedAbove true; South returns null.", "North returns null because it never falls below 1.0; South returns a crossing at the last period of the record."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The North element has been in surplus since the first month of record, so the crossing is not in the record and the flag says so. The South element never reaches a cumulative VRR of 1.0 anywhere, so there is no crossing to report. Both are honest answers to a question the data cannot otherwise settle, and they mean opposite things.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 11, 'One pattern holding all four producers with every allocation row summing to 1 reproduces the field series exactly. What does a failure of that test indicate?', '["A defect in the pattern layer itself, since the physics and the conventions are shared with the field ledger.", "A defect in the allocation matrix, since the rows would not sum correctly.", "A defect in the well classification.", "A defect in the fixture, since only a constructed dataset can satisfy the invariant exactly."]'::jsonb, 0, 'In the degenerate case the pattern ledger must reduce to the field ledger, so any disagreement is arithmetic in the new layer rather than a question about geometry or physics. The test is constructed so the matrix is trivially correct, and any correct implementation satisfies it on any dataset.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 12, 'Ekene''s two pattern injected voidages total 201937.9433211719 rb while the field''s is 229474.93559224083 rb. What accounts for the gap?', '["The formation volume factors, since pattern periods are converted at each element''s own average pressure.", "Rounding in the allocation fractions, which were rounded to the nearest 0.05.", "The overlap between the two elements, which is counted once at field level and twice at pattern level.", "The out-of-zone volume, which the pattern ledgers exclude."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Summing the pattern injections and subtracting from the field total must equal what the allocation audit booked as out of zone, because they are the same quantity computed two ways. That is one of the two checks to run on any real pattern set, the other being that the pattern productions sum exactly to the field''s.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 13, 'What does the split NOT change about the field?', '["The available actions, since a second number introduces the split as a lever.", "The pressure history, the total injected and produced voidage, and the underlying physics.", "The first hypothesis to test when pressure falls at a field VRR near 1.03.", "The place to look for problems, since an over-injected element and a starved one prompt different investigations."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The pressure track was built from field cumulative volumes and is untouched by how they are grouped, the totals are unchanged so a pure rebalance is budget-neutral, and pattern VRR uses the same voidage equation with the same conventions. The other three are exactly what the split does change.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 14, 'Ekene-5 has received 8999.01708204866 barrels over three years and has never produced water. What is the right reading?', '["It is being displaced efficiently, since it is producing oil without water breakthrough.", "It has broken through into a thief zone, since water that arrives without appearing must be leaving the pattern.", "It is not being flooded in any meaningful sense, so expecting a flood response there expects something nobody paid for.", "Its allocation share of 0.10 is too low, since a producer receiving that little would show a measurable pressure decline that the field record does not contain."]'::jsonb, 2, 'About a tenth of what the nearest producer receives, and no water at all, describes a well the flood has barely touched. The absence of water is an absence of flood rather than evidence of efficient displacement, which is the same distinction the water arrival module draws between no data and a phenomenon that has not occurred.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 15, 'Why does the recommendation scale on the rolling rather than the cumulative VRR?', '["Because the cumulative VRR is not defined per pattern, only per field.", "Because the rolling window matches the reservoir''s pressure response time.", "Because the cumulative VRR includes the early ramp, which is a field-level feature that the pattern series does not share.", "Because scaling on the cumulative asks the element to correct three years of deficit in one step."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A pattern that was badly under-injected for two years and has been on target for six months has a poor cumulative VRR and needs no change at all. On the South element the two values are close enough that using the wrong one gives a near-miss rather than an obvious failure, which is worse. The rolling window is a smoothing choice rather than a physical time constant.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 16, 'The recommended South injection is 2683.051749612857 barrels per period against a current allocated 1631.9738070945923. What is the check?', '["The result should be close to the North element''s recommendation of 2676.4681423234074, since both target 1.0.", "The scale should be above 1.6 and the result should be an increase of roughly two thirds.", "The scale should equal the reciprocal of the element''s cumulative VRR.", "The result should not exceed twice the current value, since the clamp bounds any recommendation at a scale of 2.0."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A rolling VRR of 0.6082528252875008 against a target of 1.0 gives a scale of 1.644053193714856. The two elements converge on similar rates at a target of 1.05 rather than 1.0, the cumulative is a different number from the rolling, and while the clamp does bound the scale at 2.0 that is a limit rather than a check on this answer.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 17, 'The scale rule assumes the extra water goes where the allocation says. What follows if Ekene-5''s real connection to Ekene-4 is weaker than 0.10?', '["Most of the recommended increment goes out of zone and the element''s VRR barely moves.", "The recommendation is unaffected, since the scale is applied to allocated rather than raw injection.", "The element''s VRR rises faster than predicted, since less water is credited to it in the denominator.", "The clamp engages, since a weaker connection raises the required scale above 2.0 on the next iteration."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The scale is applied to allocated injection and therefore inherits the matrix entirely, which is why a weak real connection means the water arrives in the accounting and not in the rock. Being applied to allocated volumes is the mechanism of the problem rather than a protection against it.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 18, 'The engine reports the North element''s per-injector changes as Ekene-2 down 490.99914208304875 and Ekene-4 down 152.7552886480596. Why report them separately rather than as a single element total?', '["Because the two injectors have different injectivity indices, so the same volume change costs different pressure.", "Because the allocation shares differ, so the two changes are not in the ratio 0.6 to 0.4.", "Because they are what an operator actually turns, and because the same injector appears in the other element''s recommendation with the opposite sign.", "Because the engine cannot compute an element total until both patterns have been processed, so per-injector values are the only ones available at that point."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A recommendation has to arrive as a setting on a valve. And Ekene-2 is asked to reduce for the North element and increase for the South, so a netted figure would hide the structure of the correction. The differing injectivities are real and are precisely what the advice module does NOT know about.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 19, 'The clamp bounds the scale to between 0.5 and 2.0. What does a clamped result mean?', '["That the recommendation is invalid and should be discarded.", "That the requested target is further from the current state than a single proportional step can defend.", "That the element''s rolling VRR has fallen outside the operator target band.", "That the engine has substituted the nearest deliverable rate, taking the injector''s capacity into account."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The clamped number is the boundary of the band rather than an answer to the question asked, and the flag saying so carries more information than the value. It is a guard against an unreasonable proportional step, and it knows nothing about injectivity, pump capacity or fracture pressure.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 20, 'At what target does the South element''s raw scale exceed the clamp, given a rolling VRR of 0.6082528252875008?', '["Above 1.05, which is the field''s own design target.", "Above 2.0, since the clamp bounds the scale and the target is what is being clamped.", "Above 1.2, since that is the upper bound of the operator target band and the clamp is aligned with it.", "Above 1.2165056505750016."]'::jsonb, 3, '2.0 x 0.6082528252875008 = 1.2165056505750016, so any target above that is clamped. The clamp is applied to the SCALE rather than to the target, and it is a property of the recommendation rule rather than of the operator band, which is a separate input used for flagging periods.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 21, 'The lesson gives an argument that differentiated targets can double count a belief. What is it?', '["A belief that one element is less connected belongs in the allocation matrix, which already encodes it once.", "Applying two targets means running the scale rule twice, which compounds the proportional correction.", "A differentiated target changes the rolling window as well as the scale.", "The clamp is applied per element, so two targets mean two clamps and an element can be limited twice."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Connectivity judgements live in the matrix. Encoding the same belief a second time as a lower target for that element counts it twice and puts it in two places, which makes the recommendation harder to audit. Simplicity is also worth something: one field target is easier to operate against and harder to let quietly drift.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 22, 'A Hall plot''s integral always rises, because pressure is positive. What carries the information?', '["The height of the curve at the end of the record, compared against the other injectors.", "The curvature, since a straight line means constant injectivity and any deviation is a change.", "The slope, compared against its own earlier value.", "The intercept, which recovers the reference pressure the well is injecting against and therefore the reservoir pressure at the sandface."]'::jsonb, 2, 'An eye reading the height of the curve rather than its gradient will call every well in the field a problem, and comparing heights between wells reported at different cadences is worse still. Curvature is real information but the engine reduces it to two fitted slopes, and the plot has no meaningful intercept.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 23, 'Ekene''s monthly rows give time steps of 28 to 31 days, so the Hall integrals accumulate in month-sized steps. What does that mean for comparing wells?', '["Compare slopes rather than integrals, since a well reported daily and the same well reported monthly give the same slopes and very different absolute values.", "Compare integrals rather than slopes, since the integral is smoothed and the slope is not.", "Normalise both series by the record length before comparing either.", "Compare neither directly, since the engine''s one-day floor on the time step makes monthly and daily records structurally incomparable."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Both I and W scale together with cadence, so the slope is invariant and the absolute magnitudes are not. Never compare Hall integrals between wells reported at different cadences without checking. The one-day floor guards against zero or negative gaps and does not affect a well-formed monthly record.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 24, 'Ekene-4''s above-reference Hall slopes are 2.0000000000000013 and 2.857142857142859. How should the baseline be reported?', '["As 2.0000000000000013, since anything else discards precision the engine actually delivered.", "As 2.0, while recording the raw double in the digest.", "As 2, with the trailing digits treated as evidence of a small real change in injectivity over the baseline period.", "As a range from 2.0 to 2.857142857142859, since the two thirds bracket the well''s behaviour over the record."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The trailing digits in the fourteenth place are the least squares round trip rather than a physical deviation, and the planted injectivity index is exactly 0.5. Report 2.0, record the raw value, and never key an assessment on the trailing digit. The two slopes describe two different periods rather than bracketing one.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 25, 'Why is an injectivity loss that costs no volume still worth finding?', '["Because the loss will reverse the pattern VRR calculation, which uses injected volume.", "Because a loss of injectivity always precedes a loss of containment.", "Because the volume record cannot be trusted once a well is pushing harder to hold its rate, so every downstream number becomes suspect.", "Because the alert arrives while there is still headroom, before the well starts falling short of its target."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Ekene-4 kept hitting its volume target throughout its thirty percent loss by pushing harder, which is exactly why the volume ledger shows nothing. A thirty percent loss matters once it starts costing volume or the required pressure approaches parting, and finding it early is what leaves room to act. The volume record remains perfectly accurate.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 26, 'What does the Hall slope actually measure, between the gauge and the reservoir?', '["Everything in series: the tubing, the perforations, the near-wellbore damage zone and the formation.", "The formation alone, since near-wellbore effects cancel in the ratio of two slopes.", "The near-wellbore skin alone.", "The formation and the perforations, since the tubing pressure drop is removed by referencing the pressure to 2050 psia."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A partially plugged filter upstream of the gauge changes nothing and one downstream changes the slope, so knowing where the gauge is, is part of reading the plot. Near-wellbore skin and a change far from the well look identical in p/q, and the reference subtraction removes a constant rather than a tubing term.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 27, 'The absolute-pressure Hall run on Ekene-4 gives 1.0669369155108472 and the above-reference run gives 1.4285714285714286, against a threshold of 1.2. What is the general lesson?', '["The threshold should be set per convention, so that both runs raise the same alerts.", "Absolute pressure should never be integrated.", "A convention can decide whether a problem is visible at all, so it has to travel with the number.", "A diagnostic that depends on a convention is not a diagnostic and should be replaced by a direct injectivity test."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Both readings of pressure are defensible and they disagree about whether the field has a problem. Lowering the threshold to catch the absolute run would raise false alerts across the field, since the 2050/q term varies with rate. A direct injectivity test is a good idea and does not remove the need to name conventions.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 28, 'The thirds construction is progressively blinder to changes that happen earlier in a record. Which practice does the lesson recommend to compensate?', '["Shorten the record so that the change falls in the last third.", "Find the date of the change and match it against operations records such as a workover or a change of water source.", "Fit slopes to halves rather than thirds.", "Re-run the analysis with the thirds shifted, so that a change in the middle appears in a fitted slope under at least one of the shifts."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A degradation with a date can be matched against a workover, a change of water source or a filter failure, which turns a diagnostic into an action. The lesson also asks you to read the ratio against the record length and to look at the plot before believing the two fitted numbers, since curvature and scatter carry information the ratio throws away.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 29, 'A Chan derivative with a clear positive slope on log-log axes indicates what, and why?', '["Coning, because water drawn up from below accelerates as the cone approaches the perforations.", "Normal displacement, because a front sweeping past raises the water cut steadily as it passes.", "Channelling, because water moving through a high permeability path keeps arriving in increasing proportion.", "An indeterminate result, because a positive derivative slope is consistent with all three mechanisms and only the magnitude separates them."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under channelling both the water oil ratio and its derivative keep climbing. Under coning or matrix displacement the ratio saturates as the mechanism completes, so the derivative goes flat or declines. The threshold is 0.4 for channelling and 0.0 for coning, with a transitional band between.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 30, 'Ekene-1 produces water in 6 of 36 months and is excluded from the Chan table. What kind of absence is that?', '["No data at all: the phenomenon has not occurred.", "Data excluded by a quality rule, since the six wet months failed the engine''s validation.", "A classification of indeterminate, since the engine returns null rather than omitting the well from the table.", "Not enough data yet: the phenomenon has started and the record is short."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Ekene-1 started making water in June 2025 and will be classifiable in a few more months. That is different from Ekene-5, which has zero wet months in 36 and represents a phenomenon that has not occurred, and different again from data present but rejected for bad dates or negative rates. Separating the three is the general habit.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 31, 'The Chan classifications on this fixture should be held loosely. What exactly is the concern?', '["The water histories are designed ramps, and a rising ramp read on its rising limb produces a steep derivative whether or not a channel exists.", "The 36 monthly points are too few for the log-log fit to be stable.", "The smoothing window flattens the late-time slope, biasing both producers toward the coning band.", "The classification uses allocated rather than measured water rates, so it inherits the allocation matrix along with every other pattern number."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Chan classifies from shape, and a designed ramp has a shape by construction. That caveat is exactly why the Expert tier''s volumetric back-out matters: it reaches the same conclusion using no curve shape at all. Chan works from measured producer rates and needs no allocation.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 32, 'The lag table returns identical correlations to fifteen figures for both injectors against every producer. What is the cause?', '["The engine correlates each producer against the field injection total rather than against the individual injectors.", "The two injection series are exact scalar multiples of each other, and correlation is scale invariant.", "The two injectors are close enough that their signals arrive at the producers together.", "The detrending by first differences removes the level difference between the two series, leaving them identical."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The fixed 0.6 to 0.4 split makes one series 1.5 times the other at every point, and correlation does not see a scale factor. The engine does correlate each injector separately, which is what makes the identical answers so informative, and first differencing removes a trend rather than a scale.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 33, 'The lag table''s field is called lag_days but the values are 0, 2, 3 and 6 on monthly rows. What is being reported?', '["Days, correctly, since the engine converts row indices to days using the mean month before reporting.", "Days, but computed from a mean month of 30.4375, so the values should be divided by that factor.", "Months, because the engine counts rows and the rows happen to be monthly.", "An index into the correlation array, which has no time units at all until the caller supplies the row spacing."]'::jsonb, 2, 'crossCorrelatePair steps one ROW at a time and the field name says days, so monthly data yields months labelled as days. Knowing the row spacing is the caller''s job, which is precisely the trap. The values are genuine lags rather than bare array indices.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 34, 'The planted response order is Ekene-6 at 3 months, Ekene-1 at 5, Ekene-3 at 6 and Ekene-5 at 9, while the table reports 0, 2, 3 and 6. What does the table get right?', '["The ordering of the four producers, while the absolute values come out at about half the planted times.", "The absolute values, once the row-to-month conversion is applied.", "Both the ordering and the spacing, since the reported lags are a constant offset below the planted ones.", "Nothing at all, which is why the table is described as the most instructive failure in the course."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The ranking is preserved and that is all that is preserved. The spacings do not match a constant offset, and the values are already in months rather than needing conversion. Getting the ordering right while being wrong about the magnitudes and unable to attribute an injector is exactly what makes it instructive rather than merely wrong.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 35, 'What is Chan''s diagnostic scope?', '["The severity of water production, expressed as a late-time slope that scales with the water rate.", "The source of the water, distinguishing injected water from formation water.", "The mechanism of water arrival, not its severity.", "The timing of breakthrough, since the late-time slope is fitted from the first month with a positive water oil ratio."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A well at a water oil ratio of 2 and one at 40 can share a classification, because the slope reads how the ratio is EVOLVING rather than how large it is. Severity is the ratio itself, the water rate and the cost of handling it, all separate numbers. Distinguishing injected from formation water needs a chemical analysis.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 36, 'Which two of the tier''s findings are the more robust half, and why?', '["The pattern split and the recommended correction, because both are computed from measured volumes.", "The Hall injectivity finding and the Chan water arrival finding, because neither uses the allocation matrix.", "The out-of-zone total and the conservation audit, because the audit residual is exactly zero.", "The lag table and the Chan finding, because both work from rate data rather than from volumes."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Hall uses one injector''s own pressure and rate; Chan uses one producer''s own oil and water rates. Everything at pattern level rests on eight fractions from a map and a judgement call. The audit conserves whatever matrix it is given, so a zero residual is no evidence for the matrix, and the lag table is a failure rather than a finding.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 37, 'Capstone field 1 asks for the out-of-zone injection, and there is a shortcut on this field. What is it, and why is it a bad habit?', '["Twelve percent of the total injected works here only because the split is fixed; on a field with varying splits, each injector''s own shortfall must apply to its own volume.", "Subtracting the pattern injected voidages from the field total works here only because the two elements happen to cover all four producers.", "Taking the mean of the two row shortfalls works here because they are close.", "Reading the residual of the conservation audit works here because the residual is exactly zero, which will not hold once rounding is introduced."]'::jsonb, 0, '0.6 x 0.10 + 0.4 x 0.15 = 0.12, so on Ekene the shortcut and the correct calculation agree exactly. The habit is still wrong. The pattern subtraction is in fact a legitimate second route to the same number, the mean of the shortfalls would give 0.125, and the audit residual is zero by construction rather than being the answer.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 38, 'Capstone fields 2 and 3 have a check attached. What is it?', '["Both should sit inside the operator target band of 1.00 to 1.20, since that band was chosen for this field.", "Their average should equal the field cumulative VRR of 1.034899536109.", "Their ratio should equal the ratio of the two injectors'' allocation row sums, 0.90 to 0.85, since that is what drives the imbalance.", "North should sit well above the field value and South well below, with the two produced voidages within a few percent of each other."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The two elements produce almost identical voidage, 112596.66438357021 rb against 109139.77242556904 rb, so the difference in their VRRs comes almost entirely from the injection side. South at 0.6097477559533482 sits well outside the operator band, the field value is not the mean of the two, and the row sums are far too close to explain a factor of two.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 39, 'Capstone field 4''s baseline is the allocated injection into the element, not the raw volumes of the injectors that feed it. Why does the distinction matter?', '["Because allocated volumes are in reservoir barrels while raw volumes are at the surface.", "Because the raw volumes include the injection from months before the element existed.", "Because the injectors that feed an element also feed the other one, and part of their volume goes out of zone.", "Because the allocated injection is computed on the rolling window while the raw volumes are cumulative over the whole record."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Ekene-4 sends 0.50 of its water to the South element, 0.35 to the North and books 0.15 out of zone, so its raw volume is not the element''s baseline. Both quantities are surface barrels, both cover the same periods, and the window choice applies equally to either.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 40, 'Capstone field 5''s prompt states the 2050 psia reference explicitly. What does that tell you about the field?', '["That the reference is a fitted quantity you would otherwise have to derive from the pressure data.", "That reading the prompt is part of the exercise, since the convention decides the answer.", "That the absolute-pressure run is expected, since the reference is given so it can be added back.", "That the reference varies between the two injectors, so it must be stated per well rather than assumed."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The absolute run gives 1.0669369155108472 and the above-reference run gives 1.4285714285714286, so the convention is the whole content of the field. The reference is a design input rather than something fitted, it is 2050 psia for both injectors, and it is stated so it can be SUBTRACTED.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 41, 'Capstone field 6 asks for the Chan late-time slope on Ekene-6. What is the check?', '["It should match the field aggregate of 2.2114559940777454, since Ekene-6 dominates the field water production.", "It should fall between the coning threshold of 0.0 and the channelling threshold of 0.4.", "It should equal the ratio of Ekene-6''s final water oil ratio to its first, since the late-time slope is fitted over the whole record.", "It should sit well above the 0.4 threshold and be the larger of the two producer slopes the engine returns."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Only two producers get a curve at all, and Ekene-6''s 2.348281726147951 is the larger, above Ekene-3''s 1.6028659409443355. A run returning three or four producer curves has picked up something that is not water arrival. The field aggregate is a separate and less useful number, and the slope is fitted on the derivative at late time rather than over the whole record.'),
  ('waterflood', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 42, 'How does the nature of the judgement change across the three tiers?', '["The Associate tier has objective numbers, this tier has judgement in the inputs, and the Expert tier has judgement in the model.", "Each tier removes an assumption from the one before it, so the Expert tier is the most objective of the three.", "The judgement is constant and only the volume of data changes.", "The Associate tier has judgement in its conventions, this tier removes it through the conservation audit, and the Expert tier reintroduces it through the correlations."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Associate tier counts barrels with stated conventions, this tier adds eight allocation fractions, and the Expert tier adds piston displacement, no crossflow, equal porosity per layer and piston-like areal growth. The engines surface those as warnings rather than hiding them, and reading the warnings is part of the work.');
